<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<component type="desktop-application">
  <id>online.fujinet.go.atari5200.kde</id>
  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>GPL-3.0-or-later</project_license>
  <name>FujiNet Go Atari 5200 (KDE)</name>
  <summary>Atari 5200 with a built-in FujiNet cartridge</summary>
  <description>
    <p>
      A self-contained Atari 5200 SuperSystem with a FujiNet built in: browse
      network hosts from the console, boot games and programs over the
      network, and let a FujiNet program keep talking to the network, all in
      one application.
    </p>
    <p>
      FujiNet's CONFIG program is built in and starts at power-on, exactly as
      on the FujiNet Atari 5200 cartridge. Local cartridge images (.a52, .bin,
      .rom, .car, or a zip holding one) can be opened in its place: the
      FujiNet cartridge serves them as a game, choosing the right board, and
      the console power-cycles into it. Power Cycle brings CONFIG back, as
      switching a real 5200 off and on does.
    </p>
    <p>
      The emulator is atari800, forced into 5200 mode, with the FujiNet
      cartridge running the real cartridge firmware's own protocol and
      page-mapping sources and talking to an in-process FujiNet.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Controllers window draws the 5200 controller, with its analog stick,
      its two fire buttons, its 12-key keypad and START, PAUSE and RESET;
      click and hold to press, drag the stick, and Map rebinds any key,
      direction or button to a key or a gamepad button, for all four ports.
      Gamepads are picked up as they are plugged in and out. A native debugger
      shows the 6502, disassembly with breakpoints and watchpoints, ANTIC and
      its display list, GTIA, POKEY, the memory map and every packet on the
      FujiNet cartridge's link.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing copyrighted is included: the Altirra 5200 OS is built in, so no
      BIOS is needed. Atari's own BIOS can be imported from files, a zip or a
      folder; game cartridge images are your own to open.
    </p>
  </description>
  <launchable type="desktop-id">online.fujinet.go.atari5200.kde.desktop</launchable>
  <icon type="stock">online.fujinet.go.atari5200.kde</icon>
  <!-- Software centres classify from these, not from the .desktop
       Categories= line; without them the app lands in no section at all. -->
  <categories>
    <category>Game</category>
    <category>Emulator</category>
  </categories>
